WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.880
us with your thoughts on the magnitude of SDI improvement you're hoping to show, and

00:00:04.880 --> 00:00:08.200
indeed, whether you still consider that to be the right and definitive endpoint.

00:00:08.440 --> 00:00:08.840
Thank you.

00:00:10.360 --> 00:00:11.080
Yeah, thanks, Emmanuel.

00:00:11.540 --> 00:00:17.180
So, absolutely, we saw very fast enrollment, faster than expected enrollment for inalimum

00:00:17.180 --> 00:00:17.860
in Sjogren's.

00:00:17.920 --> 00:00:20.140
We have pulled forward that readout.

00:00:20.240 --> 00:00:24.640
As you know, Sjogren's, again, a relatively prevalent rheumatological disease without

00:00:24.640 --> 00:00:27.300
really any great, good treatment options.

00:00:27.300 --> 00:00:32.880
The Phase II data for inalimum out of Sjogren's showed, you know, really the first time that

00:00:32.880 --> 00:00:39.540
you could have a significant improvement in SDI as well as other patient report outcomes.

00:00:40.040 --> 00:00:44.920
So, obviously, we won't quantitate the magnitudes of SDI benefit, but if we can repeat what

00:00:44.920 --> 00:00:49.420
we saw in Phase IIb, we think that would be really a compelling option.

00:00:49.660 --> 00:00:56.940
Also, a unique mechanism of action, anti-bath, allows you to deplete B cells in multiple

00:00:56.940 --> 00:00:57.280
components.

00:00:57.300 --> 00:01:02.920
Which we think will be important for a disease like Sjogren's, which impacts multiple different

00:01:02.920 --> 00:01:03.380
tissues.

00:01:03.920 --> 00:01:08.720
I think what will be important in addition to SDI is patient reported outcomes, meaning

00:01:08.720 --> 00:01:11.760
salivary gland function, dry eye.

00:01:12.100 --> 00:01:15.880
Many of these things, these areas are what patients would like to see improved.

00:01:16.180 --> 00:01:22.440
And if we can demonstrate, in addition to the composite endpoint, PROs that show important

00:01:22.440 --> 00:01:26.180
benefits for these patients, we think this could be an exciting opportunity.

00:01:26.600 --> 00:01:26.940
Overall, we're looking at a lot of different things.

00:01:26.940 --> 00:01:32.560
Overall, we think Ionilumab is a multi-billion dollar opportunity in combination with Sjogren's.

00:01:32.980 --> 00:01:39.580
We have multiple other Phase III programs ongoing and four separate hematological indications

00:01:39.580 --> 00:01:42.640
where we expect readouts in 2027.

00:01:43.180 --> 00:01:47.040
We also take Ionilumab into other immunology indications as well.

00:01:47.180 --> 00:01:50.940
So, this is an opportunity for us to really build a significant medicine.

00:01:51.600 --> 00:01:55.940
And as a reminder as well, this medicine has protection into the mid to late 2030s.

00:01:56.940 --> 00:01:58.440
Thank you.

00:01:58.440 --> 00:01:59.440
Next question, operator.

00:02:00.320 --> 00:02:01.320
Thank you.

00:02:01.560 --> 00:02:06.320
Your next question comes from the line of Richard Parks from BNP Paribas.

00:02:06.320 --> 00:02:07.320
Please go ahead.

00:02:09.950 --> 00:02:11.370
Hi, thank you for taking my question.

00:02:11.370 --> 00:02:14.490
I was going to stick on pipeline events in 2025.

00:02:15.330 --> 00:02:19.070
On Pelicast, I don't know, probably your next sort of multi-bot was to read out.

00:02:19.070 --> 00:02:23.870
Could you just remind us what your powering assumptions are in terms of the benefit that

00:02:23.870 --> 00:02:27.150
you're looking to see in the LP little a horizon trial?

00:02:27.150 --> 00:02:28.830
And then can you talk about balance?

00:02:28.990 --> 00:02:35.610
So, barriers to uptake of LP little a targeting agents, obviously, PCSK9 uptake's been disappointing

00:02:35.610 --> 00:02:39.070
for investors, partly attributed to the need for injections and costs.

00:02:39.070 --> 00:02:45.870
I'm just wondering if those barriers are going to also limit LP little a targeting agents,

00:02:45.870 --> 00:02:51.490
or is the lack of available alternatives for patients with elevated LP little a going

00:02:51.490 --> 00:02:54.650
to mean those barriers are less significant?

00:02:55.650 --> 00:02:57.230
So, if you could just talk about that, that would be great.

00:02:57.230 --> 00:02:57.750
Thank you.

00:02:58.990 --> 00:02:59.750
Thank you, Richard.

00:02:59.750 --> 00:03:05.490
So, first, in terms of the study design, the way we designed the Pelicast study was to

00:03:05.490 --> 00:03:08.490
look at two different levels of LP little a.

00:03:08.490 --> 00:03:15.110
So, first, the top quartile of, at the top quartile of LP little a levels, and then

00:03:15.110 --> 00:03:19.650
a separate analysis, still part of the primary endpoint, which allows us to take a second

00:03:19.650 --> 00:03:24.990
look at the top decile of patients in terms of their level of LP little a.

00:03:24.990 --> 00:03:28.530
And that was based on, you know, large-scale epidemiologic studies on how to, you know,

00:03:28.530 --> 00:03:36.030
how risk evolves from different quartiles and deciles of patients with elevated LP little a.

00:03:36.030 --> 00:03:42.910
So, our hope is to show us both of those analysis, and our goal will be to show greater than 20% CVRR.

00:03:42.910 --> 00:03:46.030
But, of course, we have aspirations to get even higher.

00:03:46.030 --> 00:03:50.330
And I think if we can show even higher levels, that would certainly create a lot of motivation

00:03:50.330 --> 00:03:53.650
in physicians to make sure these patients are tested.

00:03:53.650 --> 00:03:58.490
One of the things we've learned, I think, through our various cardiovascular launches is,

00:03:58.530 --> 00:04:04.070
in this day and age, to take more of a specialty cardiology mindset in how we think about

00:04:04.070 --> 00:04:05.290
launching these medicines.

00:04:05.290 --> 00:04:12.710
So, rather than trying to do broad-based LP little a testing for patients across large

00:04:12.710 --> 00:04:18.550
populations, to actually look very systematically, looking at large-scale data sets, and really

00:04:18.550 --> 00:04:24.890
targeting the groups, ethnicities that have the highest risk of elevated LP little a,

00:04:24.890 --> 00:04:28.030
and try to promote high levels of testing within those patient populations.

00:04:28.030 --> 00:04:35.570
And alongside that, to target specialty groups, which have a higher propensity to want to

00:04:35.570 --> 00:04:36.570
test and treat.

00:04:36.570 --> 00:04:42.330
So, those are, of course, cardiovascular specialty groups, but also, when you think about interventional

00:04:42.330 --> 00:04:47.250
cardiology, I mean, there are certain specialty groups we're learning that have a higher propensity

00:04:47.250 --> 00:04:53.890
to look for these biomarkers and then treat in order to avoid the recurrence of events,

00:04:53.890 --> 00:04:56.790
also given the push towards healthcare quality around the world.

00:04:56.790 --> 00:04:57.790
So, we're trying to take a broad-based approach.

